## What is Ruby Raider?

Ruby Raider is a generator and scaffolding gem to make UI test automation easier.

### Supported Frameworks

| Web Testing Framework | Mobile Testing Framework           |
|-----------------------|------------------------------------|
| Cucumber and Selenium | Cucumber and Appium for iOS        |
| RSpec and Selenium    | RSpec and Appium for iOS           |
| Cucumber and Watir    | Cucumber and Appium for Android    |
| RSpec and Watir       | RSpec and Appium for Android       |
|                       | Cucumber and Appium Cross-platform |
|                       | RSpec and Appium Cross-platform    |

**Optional add-on for web projects:**
- **Accessibility testing (axe)**: `--accessibility` flag

***The web tests run against the [Raider Test Store](https://raider-test-site.onrender.com/).***

***To run Appium tests, download the example [app](https://github.com/RaiderHQ/raider_test_app) and start the server:***

```bash
raider u start_appium
```

This works on all platforms (Mac OS, Linux and Windows).

## Getting Started

```bash
gem install ruby_raider
raider new [name_of_project]
```

Or with parameters:

```bash
raider new [name_of_project] -p framework:[framework] automation:[automation_type]
```

### Commands

```
raider generate         # Scaffolding commands
raider utility          # Utility commands
raider new [NAME]       # Create new project
raider version          # Show version
raider help [COMMAND]   # Describe commands
```

Shortcuts: `g` (generate), `n` (new), `u` (utility), `v` (version)

### Scaffolding

```
raider g page [NAME]      # Create page object
raider g spec [NAME]      # Create RSpec test
raider g feature [NAME]   # Create Cucumber feature
raider g steps [NAME]     # Create step definitions
raider g scaffold [NAME]  # Create page + test + steps
```

<%- if wait_helper? -%>
<%- test_cmd = rspec? ? 'rspec' : 'cucumber' -%>
## How the framework works

This section walks through the project: where things live, how a test finds an element and acts on it, and how to
<%- if examples? -%>
run the tests. Most examples come from the files in this project.
<%- else -%>
run the tests.

This project was generated without example tests, so its <%= mobile? ? 'pages' : 'pages, components' %> and tests folders are empty.
Grow it with the scaffolding commands above: `raider g scaffold checkout` writes a `Checkout` page and its
<%= rspec? ? 'spec' : 'feature and steps' %>, and `raider g page`<%= rspec? ? ' and `raider g spec`' : ', `raider g feature` and `raider g steps`' %> write one file at a time. The code examples
below show what goes in them.
<%- end -%>

Two Ruby words you will see below: a **class** is a named group of methods, and a **method** is what other
languages call a function (it starts with `def` and ends with `end`).

### Folder layout

- `config/`: the settings the tests read.
<%- if mobile? -%>
  `capabilities.yml` describes the device and the app<%= cross_platform? ? ', and `config.yml` holds the other settings' : '' %>.
  See "Settings" below.
<%- if examples? -%>
- `page_objects/pages/`: one class per screen of the app. `home.rb` is the product list and `pdp.rb` the product
  details page. A page holds the locators of its elements and the actions a test can do on that screen.
<%- else -%>
- `page_objects/pages/`: one class per screen of the app, empty for now. A page holds the locators of its elements
  and the actions a test can do on that screen.
<%- end -%>
- `page_objects/abstract/`: `page.rb`, the base class every page builds on. It gives each page the wait helper.
- `helpers/`: `driver_helper.rb` starts the Appium session, `wait_helper.rb` and `raider_element.rb` are the wait
  helper, and `video_helper.rb` records the screen<%= cross_platform? ? '. `appium_helper.rb` has `element(ios: …, android: …)`, a lookup that does not wait' : '' %>.
<%- if rspec? -%>
  `spec_helper.rb` sets up RSpec: it starts the app before each test and closes it after.
<%- if examples? -%>
- `spec/`: the tests. `pdp_page_spec.rb` opens a product and checks its "Add to Cart" button.
<%- else -%>
- `spec/`: the tests, empty for now.
<%- end -%>
<%- elsif examples? -%>
- `features/`: the tests. `home.feature` describes them in plain English, `step_definitions/home_steps.rb` holds the
  Ruby code behind each step, and `support/` starts the app (`env.rb`) and makes the helpers available to the steps
  (`world.rb`).
<%- else -%>
- `features/`: the tests, empty for now. A `.feature` file describes them in plain English, `step_definitions/` holds
  the Ruby code behind each step, and `support/` starts the app (`env.rb`) and makes the helpers available to the
  steps (`world.rb`).
<%- end -%>
<%- else -%>
  `config.yml` is described under "Settings" below.
<%- if examples? -%>
- `page_objects/pages/`: one class per page of the site. `login.rb` is the login page and `account.rb` the account
  page. A page holds the locators of its elements and the actions a test can do on that page.
- `page_objects/components/`: parts that appear on many pages. `header.rb` reads the welcome message at the top.
<%- else -%>
- `page_objects/pages/`: one class per page of the site, empty for now. A page holds the locators of its elements
  and the actions a test can do on that page.
- `page_objects/components/`: parts that appear on many pages, such as a header, empty for now.
<%- end -%>
- `page_objects/abstract/`: `page.rb` and `component.rb`, the base classes every page and component builds on.
  `page.rb` gives each page the wait helper.
- `helpers/`: `driver_helper.rb` starts the browser, `wait_helper.rb` and `raider_element.rb` are the wait helper,
  `video_helper.rb` records failure videos and `session_state.rb` reuses the login.
<%- if rspec? -%>
  `spec_helper.rb` sets up RSpec: it opens the browser before each test and closes it after.
<%- end -%>
<%- if examples? -%>
- `models/`: test data. `data/users.yml` holds the users, and `model_factory.rb`<%= cucumber? ? ' and `user_factory.rb` load' : ' loads' %> them.
<%- end -%>
<%- if rspec? && examples? -%>
- `spec/`: the tests. `login_page_spec.rb` logs in with the right password and with a wrong one.
<%- elsif rspec? -%>
- `spec/`: the tests, empty for now.
<%- elsif examples? -%>
- `features/`: the tests. `login.feature` describes them in plain English, `step_definitions/login_steps.rb` holds
  the Ruby code behind each step, and `support/` opens the browser (`env.rb`) and makes the helpers available to the
  steps (`world.rb`).
<%- else -%>
- `features/`: the tests, empty for now. A `.feature` file describes them in plain English, `step_definitions/`
  holds the Ruby code behind each step, and `support/` opens the browser (`env.rb`) and makes the helpers available
  to the steps (`world.rb`).
<%- end -%>
<%- end -%>
<%- if azure? && !mobile? -%>
- `azure-pipelines.yml`: the CI pipeline.
<%- elsif !azure? -%>
- `.github/workflows/`: the CI pipeline.
<%- end -%>

### Settings

<%- if mobile? -%>
`config/capabilities.yml` tells Appium which device to use and which app to install: `platformName`,
`platformVersion`, `deviceName`, `automationName` (`XCUITest` on iOS, `UiAutomator2` on Android) and `app`, the
path to the app file. The `browserstack` part is used instead when you run with `TEST_ENV=browserstack`.

<%- if cross_platform? -%>
`config/config.yml`:

- `platform`: which part of `capabilities.yml` to use, `ios` or `android`.
- `timeout`: how many seconds the wait helper waits for an element before the test fails (10). To wait longer,
  change it, for example to `timeout: 15`.
- `video`: failure videos, see [Video replay](#video-replay).
<%- else -%>
This project has no `config/config.yml`, so the wait helper waits up to 10 seconds for an element before the test
fails. To change that, pass `timeout:` to one call (see below), or create `config/config.yml` with this line:

```yaml
timeout: 15
```
<%- end -%>
<%- else -%>
`config/config.yml`:

- `browser`: the browser the tests run in, `chrome` or `firefox`.
<%- if examples? -%>
- `url`: the address of the site under test. Each page adds its own path to it (`Login` adds
  `index.php?rt=account/login`).
<%- else -%>
- `url`: the address of the site under test. Each page adds its own path to it. Change it to your site.
<%- end -%>
- `timeout`: how many seconds the wait helper waits for an element before the test fails (10).
- `viewport`: the browser window's width and height.
- `browser_arguments`: extra options passed to each browser when it starts.
- `headless`: not in the file by default. Add `headless: true`, or set `HEADLESS=true` for one run, to run the
  browser without a window.
- `video`: failure videos, see [Video replay](#video-replay).
- `login_state`: how long a saved login is reused, see [Reusing the login](#reusing-the-login).
<%- end -%>

### Pages and components

A page keeps two kinds of methods: **elements**, which say where something is, and **actions**, which do something
<%- if examples? -%>
with it. From `page_objects/pages/<%= mobile? ? 'home.rb' : 'login.rb' %>`:
<%- else -%>
with it. For example, on a <%= mobile? ? 'product list screen' : 'login page' %>:
<%- end -%>

```ruby
<%- if mobile? -%>
def go_to_product_detail
  tap(first_product)
end

def first_product
<%- if cross_platform? -%>
  { ios: { accessibility_id: 'product-item-1' },
    android: { accessibility_id: 'product-item-1' } }
<%- else -%>
  { accessibility_id: 'product-item-1' }
<%- end -%>
end
<%- else -%>
def login(username, password)
  send_keys(username_field, username)
  send_keys(password_field, password)
  click(login_button)
end

def username_field
  { id: 'loginFrm_loginname' }
end
<%- end -%>
```

<%- if mobile? -%>
An element method returns a **locator**: a Ruby hash (a key and a value in curly braces) that tells Appium how to
find the element. `{ accessibility_id: 'product-item-1' }` means "the element whose accessibility id is
`product-item-1`". `xpath`, `id` and `class_name` work too.
<%- if cross_platform? -%>

Because this project runs on both iOS and Android, a locator can name one for each: `{ ios: { … }, android: { … } }`.
The wait helper picks the one for the device the test runs on.
<%- end -%>

The action, `tap(first_product)`, comes from the wait helper: it waits until the product is ready, then taps it.
<%- else -%>
An element method returns a **locator**: a Ruby hash (a key and a value in curly braces) that tells Selenium how to
find the element. `{ id: 'loginFrm_loginname' }` means "the element whose id is `loginFrm_loginname`". `css`,
`xpath`, `name`, `class` and `link_text` work too.

The actions, `send_keys` and `click`, come from the wait helper: each one waits until the element is ready, then
types or clicks.

<%- if examples? -%>
A **component** is a part of the page that has its own methods. `page.rb` finds the welcome message and hands it to
the `Header` component:
<%- else -%>
A **component** is a part of the page that has its own methods. It builds on `page_objects/abstract/component.rb`,
and a page hands it the element it wraps. For a `Header` component in `page_objects/components/`:
<%- end -%>

```ruby
def header
  Header.new(find_element(css: '.welcome-msg'))
end
```

`Header#customer_name` then reads its text, so a test can write `account_page.header.customer_name`.
<%- end -%>

### The wait helper and RaiderElement

<%= mobile? ? 'Apps' : 'Pages' %> load at their own speed, so a test that acts too early fails. The wait helper,
`helpers/wait_helper.rb`, waits for an element to be ready before it acts. It checks every 0.1 seconds, for up to
`timeout` seconds. If the element is still not ready, the test fails and says what it saw last:

```
<%- if mobile? -%>
Waited 10s for {accessibility_id: "product-item-1"} to be visible, enabled and stable; last seen: not found
<%- else -%>
Waited 10s for {id: "loginFrm_loginname"} to be visible and enabled; last seen: present but not visible
<%- end -%>
```

You can use its methods in every page and in every <%= rspec? ? 'test' : 'step definition' %>:

| Method | Waits until the element is | Then |
|--------|----------------------------|------|
| `find_element(locator)` | present and visible (`visible: false` skips visible) | returns it as a RaiderElement |
| `find_elements(locator)` | present, at least one | returns a list of RaiderElements, or `[]` if none showed up |
<%- if mobile? -%>
| `click(locator)`, `tap(locator)` | visible, enabled and still | taps it (`tap` and `click` do the same) |
| `send_keys(locator, 'text')` | visible and enabled | types the text |
| `clear(locator)` | visible and enabled | empties the field |
| `swipe('left', locator)` | visible | swipes on it: `'up'`, `'down'`, `'left'` or `'right'` |
| `scroll('down', locator)` | visible | scrolls inside it |
| `scroll_to(locator)` | visible, scrolling down up to 10 times | returns it as a RaiderElement |
<%- else -%>
| `click(locator)` | visible, enabled and still | clicks it |
| `send_keys(locator, 'text')` | visible and enabled | types the text |
| `clear(locator)` | visible and enabled | empties the field |
| `submit(locator)` | visible and enabled | submits its form |
| `select_by(locator, :text, 'Blue')` | visible and enabled | picks a dropdown option by `:text`, `:value` or `:index` |
| `hover(locator)` | visible and still | moves the mouse over it |
| `drag_and_drop(source, target)` | source visible, enabled and still; target visible | drags the source onto the target |
| `scroll_into_view(locator)` | present | scrolls the page to it |
<%- end -%>
| `text(locator)`, `attribute(locator, 'name')` | present | returns its text or the attribute's value |
| `displayed?(locator)`, `enabled?(locator)`, `selected?(locator)` | present | returns `true` or `false` |
| `eventually { … }` | (see below) | retries the block until it passes |

Good to know:

<%- if mobile? -%>
- Write the locator with or without braces: `tap(accessibility_id: 'login')` and `tap({ accessibility_id: 'login' })`
  are the same. When a method takes more than the locator, put the locator in braces:
  `send_keys({ accessibility_id: 'email' }, 'me@example.com')`, `swipe('left', { accessibility_id: 'card' })`.
<%- else -%>
- Write the locator with or without braces: `click(id: 'login')` and `click({ id: 'login' })` are the same. When a
  method takes more than the locator, put the locator in braces: `send_keys({ id: 'email' }, 'me@example.com')`.
<%- end -%>
- `timeout:` changes the wait for one call, in seconds: `<%= mobile? ? "tap({ accessibility_id: 'slow-button' }, timeout: 30)" : "click({ id: 'slow-button' }, timeout: 30)" %>`.
- "Still" (`stable` in error messages) means the element was in the same place on two checks in a row, 0.1 seconds
  apart, so it is not in the middle of an animation. That is why <%= mobile? ? '`click` and `tap`' : '`click`, `hover` and `drag_and_drop`' %> need at least two checks, and `timeout: 0` never passes for them.
- `text`, `displayed?` and the other readers fail when the element never shows up. To check that something is
  **not** on the <%= mobile? ? 'screen' : 'page' %>, ask for the list without waiting and check that it is empty:

  ```ruby
  eventually { expect(find_elements(<%= mobile? ? "accessibility_id: 'error'" : "css: '.error'" %>, timeout: 0)).to be_empty }
  ```
<%- if mobile? -%>
- `swipe` and `scroll` with a locator wait for that element to be visible and act on it. Without a locator
  (`swipe('up')`) they act on the whole screen and return `nil`.
- `scroll_to(locator)` checks for the element, scrolls once if it is not visible, and checks again, up to
  `max_scrolls` times (10), then fails. Change the direction with `direction: 'up'`. An element that appears late
  near the top can be scrolled past, so first wait for something on the screen, for example with `find_element`.
- `tap` with a block and no locator (`page.tap { |p| … }`) is still Ruby's own `tap`, which passes the object to the
  block.
- `hide_keyboard`, `back` and the other Appium commands are on `driver`, and do not wait.
<%- end -%>

`find_element` returns a **RaiderElement**: the element, plus the same waiting methods without the locator. So
calls chain, and each step waits:

```ruby
<%- if mobile? -%>
find_element(accessibility_id: 'list').find_element(accessibility_id: 'row').tap
find_element(accessibility_id: 'card').swipe('left')
<%- else -%>
find_element(css: '.form').find_element(id: 'email').send_keys('me@example.com')
<%- end -%>
```

A `find_element` on a RaiderElement searches only inside it. If the <%= mobile? ? 'app' : 'page' %> redraws the element, the RaiderElement
finds it again by its locator. Any other <%= mobile? ? 'Appium' : 'Selenium' %> method (`tag_name`, `rect`, `location`, …) goes straight to the
element, without waiting.

A RaiderElement is not a <%= mobile? ? 'raw Appium' : 'Selenium' %> element, so the <%= mobile? ? 'Appium' : 'Selenium' %> calls that check what they are given need
the real one, which `.native` returns:

```ruby
<%- if mobile? -%>
driver.action.click(find_element(accessibility_id: 'card').native).perform
<%- else -%>
driver.switch_to.frame(find_element(css: 'iframe').native)
Selenium::WebDriver::Support::Select.new(find_element(id: 'color').native)
driver.action.move_to(find_element(id: 'menu').native).perform
driver.execute_script('arguments[0].click()', find_element(id: 'go').native)
<%- end -%>
```
<%- if mobile? -%>

Elements you pass to `execute_script` need `.native` too.
<%- end -%>

### eventually

`eventually { … }` runs the block again and again until it passes, or until `timeout` seconds have gone by. It
retries a failed `expect`, an element that was not found, and an element that went stale (was redrawn). It returns
what the block returns, and `eventually(timeout: 30) { … }` waits longer.

Use it for checks on something that changes after an action. Before, the check runs once, and fails if the
<%= mobile? ? 'screen' : 'page' %> has not updated yet:

```ruby
<%- if mobile? -%>
expect(pdp_page.add_to_cart_text).to eq 'Add to Cart'
<%- else -%>
expect(account_page.header.customer_name).to eq "Welcome back #{user['name']}"
<%- end -%>
```

After, it is retried until the text matches:

```ruby
<%- if mobile? -%>
eventually { expect(pdp_page.add_to_cart_text).to eq 'Add to Cart' }
<%- else -%>
eventually { expect(account_page.header.customer_name).to eq "Welcome back #{user['name']}" }
<%- end -%>
```

If it still fails at the end, the error starts with `(still failing after 10s)`, followed by the last failure. The
waits inside the block share its time limit, so the whole block never takes longer than `timeout`.

### Running the tests

<%- if mobile? -%>
Start Appium and a simulator or emulator first (see "Setting up Appium" below). Then:
<%- else -%>
Install the gems once with `bundle install`. Then:
<%- end -%>

```bash
<%- if rspec? -%>
bundle exec rspec                                 # every test
bundle exec rspec spec/<%= examples? ? (mobile? ? 'pdp_page_spec.rb' : 'login_page_spec.rb') : 'checkout_page_spec.rb' %>          # one file
<%- else -%>
bundle exec cucumber                              # every scenario
bundle exec cucumber features/<%= examples? ? (mobile? ? 'home.feature' : 'login.feature') : 'checkout.feature' %>         # one feature
<%- end -%>
<%- unless mobile? -%>
HEADLESS=true bundle exec <%= test_cmd %>                   # without a browser window
<%- end -%>
```

<%- if mobile? -%>
- To run tests in parallel, each process needs its own device or simulator. This project does not set that up, so
  run the tests one at a time unless you add it.
<%- else -%>
- In parallel, with the `parallel_tests` gem in the Gemfile: `bundle exec <%= rspec? ? 'parallel_rspec spec/' : 'parallel_cucumber features/' %>`. Each process opens its own
  browser.
<%- end -%>
<%- if rspec? -%>
- `RETRY_COUNT=2 bundle exec rspec` runs each test up to 2 times, so a failing test gets one more try before it
  counts as failed.
<%- else -%>
- To run a failing scenario again before it counts as failed, change `--retry 0` in `cucumber.yml` to, for
  example, `--retry 2`.
<%- end -%>

### Running in CI

<%- if azure? && mobile? -%>
There is no Azure pipeline for mobile projects yet; see [CI](#ci).
<%- elsif azure? -%>
`azure-pipelines.yml` runs the suite headless on Azure Pipelines; see [Azure Pipelines](#azure-pipelines) to set it up.
<%- elsif mobile? -%>
`.github/workflows/appium_pipeline.yml` runs the suite on GitHub Actions on every push to `main` or `master` and on
every pull request. It downloads the test app and starts a simulator or emulator and Appium.
<%- else -%>
`.github/workflows/test_pipeline.yml` runs the suite headless on GitHub Actions on every push to `main` or `master`
and on every pull request, and uploads `raider-results/`.
<%- end -%>

Every run writes a report; see [Reporting](#reporting).
<%- if web? -%>
To log in once and reuse the session across tests, see [Reusing the login](#reusing-the-login).
<%- end -%>
<%- if mobile? -%>

### Setting up Appium

1. Install Appium and its driver: `npm install -g appium`, then
   `appium driver install <%= android? ? 'uiautomator2' : 'xcuitest' %>`<%= cross_platform? ? ' and `appium driver install uiautomator2`' : '' %>.
2. Download the example [app](https://github.com/RaiderHQ/raider_test_app) and put it in the project folder.
   `app` in `config/capabilities.yml` is its path<%= cross_platform? ? ' (`RaiderTestApp.app` for iOS, `app-release.apk` for Android)' : (ios? ? ' (`RaiderTestApp.app`)' : ' (`app-release.apk`)') %>.
3. Start a<%= android? ? 'n Android emulator' : (ios? ? 'n iOS simulator' : ' simulator or an emulator') %> that matches `deviceName` and `platformVersion`.
4. Start the Appium server with `raider u start_appium`, and keep it running while the tests run.
<%- end -%>

### Removing the wait helper

The wait helper is ordinary code in your project, so you can take it out. To do that:

1. Delete `helpers/wait_helper.rb` and `helpers/raider_element.rb`.
2. Remove `include WaitHelper` from `page_objects/abstract/page.rb`, <%= rspec? ? '`config.include(WaitHelper)` from `helpers/spec_helper.rb`' : '`World(WaitHelper)` from `features/support/world.rb`' %>, and the
   `require_relative` lines that load `wait_helper`.
3. Replace the calls:
   - `find_element(loc)` with `driver.find_element(**loc)`;
   - `click(loc)` and `send_keys(loc, …)` with `driver.find_element(**loc).click` and `.send_keys(…)`;
   - `eventually { … }` with `Selenium::WebDriver::Wait.new(timeout: 10).until { … }`, or a plain `expect`.
<%- if cross_platform? -%>
   - Per-OS locators (`{ ios: { … }, android: { … } }`) with `element(ios: { … }, android: { … })` from
     `helpers/appium_helper.rb`.
<%- end -%>
<%- if mobile? -%>
4. Optionally turn on Appium's implicit wait, so plain lookups wait for the element to be present: in
   `configure_driver` in `helpers/driver_helper.rb`, pass `appium_lib: { wait: 10 }` next to `caps:` in
   `Appium::Driver.new`.
<%- else -%>
4. Optionally put back an implicit wait, `driver.manage.timeouts.implicit_wait = 10`, in `create_driver` in
   `helpers/driver_helper.rb`, so plain lookups wait for the element to be present.
<%- end -%>
5. Scaffolding (`raider g page`, `raider g spec` and the others) then writes plain `driver.find_element` examples on
   its own, because it checks for `helpers/wait_helper.rb`.

<%- end -%>
<%- if web? -%>
## Reusing the login

<%- if examples? -%>
Tag an example `:logged_in` (RSpec) or a scenario `@logged_in` (Cucumber) and the suite logs in once, saves the
session, and restores it for the next tagged example or run instead of filling in the login form again. The generated
account example shows it. If the saved session no longer works, the real login runs again and the state is saved anew.
<%- else -%>
`helpers/session_state.rb` logs in once, saves the session, and restores it for the next test or run instead of
filling in the login form again. If the saved session no longer works, the real login runs again and the state is
saved anew.

This project has no login page yet. Once you have one (`raider g page login`), add a hook so that every
<%= rspec? ? 'example tagged `:logged_in`' : 'scenario tagged `@logged_in`' %> starts logged in. In `<%= rspec? ? 'helpers/spec_helper.rb' : 'features/support/env.rb' %>`, require your pages next to
the other `require_relative` lines, then add the hook<%= rspec? ? ' inside `RSpec.configure`' : '' %>:

```ruby
<%- if rspec? -%>
require_relative '../page_objects/pages/login'
require_relative '../page_objects/pages/dashboard'

config.before(:each, :logged_in) do
<%- else -%>
require_relative '../../page_objects/pages/login'
require_relative '../../page_objects/pages/dashboard'

Before('@logged_in') do
<%- end -%>
  dashboard = Dashboard.new(driver)
  SessionState.new(driver).reuse(valid: -> { dashboard.tap(&:visit).logged_in? }) do
    login = Login.new(driver)
    login.visit
    login.login(ENV.fetch('TEST_USER'), ENV.fetch('TEST_PASSWORD'))
  end
end
```

`Login` and `Dashboard` stand for your own pages: `valid:` checks that a restored session is still logged in (here a
`logged_in?` method you write on the page), and the block does the real login.
<%- end -%>

For another role, call it yourself with its own name:

```ruby
SessionState.new(driver, name: 'admin').reuse(valid: -> { dashboard.tap(&:visit).admin? }) do
  # the real login for the admin
end
```

With Watir, pass `browser.driver`, the Selenium driver underneath the browser.

- `login_state: max_age:` in `config/config.yml` is how many seconds a saved login stays valid (default 3600).
- `RAIDER_LOGIN_STATE=off` always does the real login and saves nothing; `RAIDER_LOGIN_STATE=refresh` ignores the saved
  file, logs in and saves again.
- The state lives in `.raider/auth/` and is git-ignored. It holds live session tokens, so it must not be committed,
  cached or uploaded as a CI artifact. Nothing in it is ever printed.

<%- end -%>
## Reporting

<%- if rspec? -%>
Every run writes machine-readable results to `raider-results/` (including `run.json`) and a browsable report,
`raider-results/index.html`. Open it in a browser after a run; `bundle exec raider-reporter report` rebuilds it.
<%- else -%>
Every run writes machine-readable results to `raider-results/` (including `run.json`) and a browsable report,
`raider-results/index.html`. The reporter runs in plugin mode:
`features/support/env.rb` requires `raider_reporter/cucumber`, so no extra Cucumber formatter flag is needed.
<%- end -%>

To upload runs to Raider, set these environment variables before running the suite.
`RAIDER_TOKEN` and `RAIDER_ENDPOINT` come from your Raider Hideout server (its setup page, or `bin/rails hideout:setup` /
`hideout:token`):

```bash
export RAIDER_TOKEN=your-token
export RAIDER_ENDPOINT=https://hideout.example.com
```

<%- if rspec? -%>
Without `RAIDER_TOKEN`, nothing is uploaded and the suite output is unchanged. Failure screenshots are attached to the
report and to Raider. See the [raider_reporter README](https://github.com/RaiderHQ/raider_reporter) for details.
<%- else -%>
Without `RAIDER_TOKEN`, nothing is uploaded and the suite output is unchanged. Failure screenshots are attached to the
report and to Raider. See the [raider_reporter README](https://github.com/RaiderHQ/raider_reporter) for details.
<%- end -%>

### Video replay

Every test is recorded, and the video is kept only for failing tests<%= rspec? ? ' (on the last retry attempt)' : '' %>. It is attached
to the report as `video` and saved under `raider-results/attachments/`, next to the failure screenshot.
<%- if mobile? -%>
Appium records the device screen and the video is attached as `video.mp4`.
<%- else -%>
The video is a `video.webm` built from page screenshots with ffmpeg.
<%- end -%>

<%- if single_platform? -%>
- Turn video off for one run with `VIDEO=off bundle exec <%= rspec? ? 'rspec' : 'cucumber' %>`, or for the project with
  `raider u video off`, which creates `config/config.yml` with a `video:` section (`raider u video on` turns it back
  on).
<%- else -%>
- Video is on by default: `video:` in `config/config.yml` (`enabled: true`, `fps: 5`, `max_width: 1280`).
- Turn it off for one run with `VIDEO=off bundle exec <%= rspec? ? 'rspec' : 'cucumber' %>`, or for the project with
  `raider u video off` (`raider u video on` turns it back on).
<%- end -%>
<%- unless mobile? -%>
- ffmpeg is downloaded on first use, checked against a pinned SHA-256 and cached in `~/.cache/raider`
  (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\raider` on Windows). `raider u video install` downloads it ahead of time (CI does this before the
  tests and caches the folder), and `raider u video status` prints the binary in use.
- `RAIDER_FFMPEG_PATH` uses your own ffmpeg (it needs libvpx) instead of the download, and `RAIDER_CACHE_DIR` moves
  the cache.
<%- if azure? -%>
- `azure-pipelines.yml` sets `RAIDER_CACHE_DIR` to `$(Pipeline.Workspace)/.cache/raider`, the folder its `Cache@2`
  step keeps between runs.
<%- end -%>
- The RaiderHQ `ffmpeg-7.1` release that the download comes from is not published yet. Until it is, set
  `RAIDER_FFMPEG_PATH` to get videos; without it the suite runs as usual and prints one `[video]` line.
- If ffmpeg is missing or recording fails, the suite still runs and its exit code is unchanged; you get one `[video]`
  line on stderr and no video.
- Known limits: a frame is taken after each page load, click and text change, plus one at the end of a failing test,
  so changes the page makes on its own between actions show up in the next frame. There is one recorded driver per
  process.
<%- else -%>
- Appium's screen recording needs `ffmpeg` on the Appium server host's `PATH` (this machine only when Appium runs
  here): always for iOS (XCUITest), and for Android when a recording is longer than 180 s. Without it, recording
  fails with one `[video]` line and the tests run as usual.
<%- if cross_platform? -%>
- Each test records for at most 1800 s on iOS and 180 s on Android, so Android needs no ffmpeg on the Appium host.
<%- elsif ios? -%>
- Each test records for at most 1800 s.
<%- else -%>
- Each test records for at most 180 s, so the Appium host needs no ffmpeg; longer tests keep only their first 180 s.
<%- end -%>
  Set `time_limit` (seconds) under `video:` in `config/config.yml`<%= single_platform? ? ' (create it if it is missing)' : '' %> to change it; on Android, more
  than 180 s needs ffmpeg on the Appium host.
- `raider u video install` and `raider u video status` manage the ffmpeg that web projects use, cached in
  `~/.cache/raider` (override with `RAIDER_FFMPEG_PATH` or `RAIDER_CACHE_DIR`); they do not install ffmpeg on the
  Appium host. The RaiderHQ `ffmpeg-7.1` release it comes from is not published yet.
- If recording fails, the suite still runs and its exit code is unchanged; you get one `[video]` line on stderr and
  no video.
<%- end -%>

<%- unless azure? && mobile? -%>
### Quarantine

CI runs the suite through `bundle exec raider-reporter exec -- …` with `RAIDER_QUARANTINE` set. Tests quarantined in
Raider still run and are still reported (as `quarantined`), and a build whose only failures are quarantined stays green.
Local runs keep quarantine off. To turn it off in CI, remove `RAIDER_QUARANTINE` from the pipeline.

<%- end -%>
<%- unless azure? && mobile? -%>
### Quality gate

The `<%= azure? ? 'raider_gate' : 'raider-gate' %>` CI job runs `bundle exec raider-reporter gate` after the tests and
fails the pipeline when the Raider quality gate fails. It is off until you turn it on:

- set the CI variable `RAIDER_GATE=1` (<%= azure? ? 'a pipeline variable' : 'a repository variable under Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → Variables' %>);
- turn on rules under the project's Gate page in Raider: a minimum pass rate, no new failures against the default
  branch, no new flaky tests;
- optionally require the `raider/gate` check in branch protection.

`raider-reporter gate` exits 1 when the gate fails, naming the failed rules, and 2 when it gets no verdict (timeout,
Raider unreachable, token rejected, build not found). `RAIDER_GATE_TIMEOUT` sets how long it waits, in seconds
(default 600). The job stays off by default because a project without `RAIDER_TOKEN`, or a pull request from a fork,
would exit 2 on every run.

Parallel test jobs that upload to one build must each set `RAIDER_SHARD_TOTAL` (the number of jobs) and
`RAIDER_SHARD_INDEX` (1 to that number), so the gate waits for all of them instead of judging the first one to finish.

<%- end -%>
<%- if azure? && mobile? -%>
## CI

Azure Pipelines template for mobile projects is not available yet. Use GitHub Actions (`ci:github`) or write your own pipeline; see the raider_reporter README for the Azure setup.

<%- elsif azure? -%>
## Azure Pipelines

`azure-pipelines.yml` runs the suite on `ubuntu-latest` and, on pull requests, comments the Raider results.

- Create a pipeline from `azure-pipelines.yml`, then add `RAIDER_TOKEN` (mark it secret) and `RAIDER_ENDPOINT` as pipeline variables.
- Contribute to pull requests: grant the pipeline's build service identity (`<Project> Build Service (<Organization>)`) the "Contribute to pull requests" permission on the repository, so `raider-reporter comment` can post the comment and status.
- Azure Repos ignores `pr:` triggers. Add this pipeline as a Build validation branch policy so pull request builds (and the Raider comment) run.
- Keep the `raider_comment` job in the same stage as the `tests` job.
- Pull request comments work for Azure Repos only; a GitHub-hosted repo built by Azure Pipelines gets results uploaded but no PR comment.
- Self-hosted agent? Replace `vmImage: ubuntu-latest` with `pool: { name: Default }`.

<%- end -%>
